The web site allows users to create accounts.  It also clears these accounts
after 5 minutes.  To dump the credentials of an account, you must supply
a valid username and password.  In both checkCredentials() and validUser(), 
there is this line after the select that determines if you get access:
    if(mysql_num_rows($res) > 0){ 
        return True; 
    } 
In dumpData(), there is this line that determines what gets retrieved:
	if(mysql_num_rows($res) > 0) { 
            while ($row = mysql_fetch_assoc($res)) { 
                return print_r($row); 
            } 
        } 
It's as if they coded it thinking there would be multiple rows returned
rather than exactly one.  So, if one were to be able to insert a duplicate 
user, then knowing the credentials of the duplicate user will dump the 
other user.  The goal is to try to then win a race condition every 5 minutes
by attempting to create a bogus natas28 user before the system repopulates
it with its own natas28 user.  We assume that the system doesn't check to
see of a natas28 user already exists in the database before blindly inserting
the target account.  After correctly timing a torrent of requests on the
5 minute mark, we eventually get the additional credential.

---
An alternative solution would be adding another natas28 account via abusing
mysql truncation. The column 'username' has a character limit of 64. If we supply
a username with a longer character count, it will get truncated. 

Entering "natas28                                                         a"
(natas28 followed by 57 spaces followed by an "a") results in the "a" being
truncated off. The whitespace then gets trimed off. The result is that we
are able to create another account with the username "natas28".

Finally, we can log on to the natas28 account using whatever password we
just supplied.

